        <h1>INTRODUCTION TO SPACE</h1>
        <p>Space is the endless area that starts where Earth's atmosphere ends. It is dark, silent, and very cold. Space contains everything - planets, stars, moons, galaxies, and black holes. Space is about 13.8 billion years old. The study of space is called<b>Astronomy</b>.</p>
        <h2>OUR SOLAR SYSTEM</h2>
        <p>We live in the Solar System. In the center is the Sun, which is a big star. It gives us light and heat. Without the Sun, there would be no life. Around the Sun, 8 planets move in fixed paths called orbits.</p>
        <h3>THE 8 PLANETS OF OUR SOLAR SYSTEM</h3>
        <p><b>1. Mercury:</b>It is the smallest planet and closest to the Sun. It has no atmosphere and is very fast. A year on Mercury is only 88 days.</p>
        <p><b>2. Venus:</b> The second planet. It is the hottest planet (462°C) because of thick gases. It is called Earth's twin.</p>
        <p><b>3. Earth:</b>Our home planet. It is the only planet where life exists. 71% of Earth is water. It has one moon.</p>
        <p><b>4. Mars:</b>Known as the Red Planet because of its red soil. Scientists believe there was once water on Mars. NASA sends rovers to Mars.</p>
        <p><b>5. Jupiter:</b>The biggest planet in our solar system. It is made of gas and has a giant red storm.</p>
        <p><b>6. Saturn:</b>Famous for its beautiful rings made of ice and rock. It has more than 80 moons.</p>
        <p><b>7. Uranus:</b>An ice giant that rotates sideways. It has a blue-green color.</p>
        <p><b>8. Neptune:</b>The farthest and windiest planet. It is dark blue and very cold.</p>
        
